# Line endings for files whose BYTES are compared, not just their text.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. This machine has core.autocrlf=true set globally. That is fine for source --
# the compiler does not care -- but this repository has fixtures that are checked with a BYTE
# comparison, because that is the only way to catch two implementations of a format drifting apart
# (see OcGraph.hpp's own account of the C++ and C# readers silently disagreeing about what a graph
# returns). autocrlf rewrites those fixtures on checkout, so the bytes on disk stop matching the
# bytes in the object database and the comparison fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the
# code.
#
# It showed up as a phantom: a fresh `git worktree add` got CRLF copies (632 bytes) while the
# original checkout still held the LF blob (605 bytes), so OcGraphTest and GraphEditorLoadSaveTest
# failed in the worktree and passed in the main tree, on identical source. Half an hour of an
# adversarial reviewer's time went into proving that was an artifact rather than a regression.
#
# Pinning the format fixtures to LF makes the byte comparison mean what it says everywhere.
*.ocgraph  text eol=lf
*.ocmat    text eol=lf
*.ocmap    text eol=lf
*.ocproject text eol=lf
